@*
    What runs on a write.

    A before-hook and an after-hook are not the same editor and must not look like one: the frozen
    schema's `$defs/beforeHookList` admits `reject` and `mutate` only — "no network, no external
    calls" — because a before-hook runs inside the write's own transaction. An after-hook is where
    a webhook or an email belongs. The point's name is therefore shown with what it may contain,
    rather than the two being flattened into one list of "actions".

    All six points are honoured by this build — PR5a landed the three `after*`, PR5b the three
    `before*` — which is what unblocked editing them at all. What is still refused is three of the
    five *action types*, and those are read off `capabilities` rather than listed here, so the day
    one of them lands the tab stops warning about it without anybody editing this file.
*@

<SectionHead Title="On write">
    <Subtitle>
        Before-hooks run inside the transaction and can only refuse or change the row.
        After-hooks run once it is committed and are where anything that leaves the process belongs.
    </Subtitle>
</SectionHead>

@*
    Folded by default. Three refusals at full length were the first screen of this tab on a phone — the
    operator scrolled past a wall to reach what the entity actually declares. Shut, it is one line that
    still says the count and the names; the wording inside is the framework's own, unshortened.
*@
@if (ActionRefusals.Count > 0)
{
    <details class="a-refused a-refused--fold" data-testid="hooks-refused">
        <summary>
            @ActionRefusals.Count action type@(ActionRefusals.Count == 1 ? "" : "s") this build declares and does not run —
            <span class="a-mono">@string.Join(", ", ActionRefusals.Select(refusal => refusal.Slot))</span>
        </summary>
        @foreach (var refusal in ActionRefusals)
        {
            <span class="a-refused__reason">@refusal.Consequence</span>
        }
    </details>
}

@if (Hooks.Count == 0)
{
    <EmptyState Title="Nothing runs on a write to this entity"
                Body="No hooks are declared. Writes are validated against the field facets and the rules, and nothing else happens." />
}
else
{
    @foreach (var point in Hooks)
    {
        <ListRow class="a-listrow--roomy a-listrow--stacked">
            <div class="a-row">
                <span class="@($"a-badge {(HookBuilder.IsBefore(point.Key) ? "a-badge--warn" : "a-badge--accent")}")">@point.Key</span>
                <span class="a-section__sub">
                    @(HookBuilder.IsBefore(point.Key)
                        ? "in the transaction · may refuse or change the row · no network"
                        : "after the commit · may leave the process")
                </span>
            </div>

            @{
                var declared = Declared(point.Value);
                var name = point.Key;
            }

            @for (var position = 0; position < declared.Count; position++)
            {
                var hook = declared[position];
                var at = position;

                @*
                    The control sits under the declaration on a phone and beside it on a desktop. Beside it
                    at 375 px, the code block gave up a third of the row to one word and every condition
                    wrapped mid-token.
                *@
                <div class="a-hookrow" data-testid="hook-row">
                    @if (Staged.IsNewHook(name, at))
                    {
                        <span class="a-badge a-badge--accent a-hookrow__badge"
                              data-testid="hook-staged">new</span>
                    }
                    <CodeBlock Json="@hook" />
                    @if (Editable)
                    {
                        <button type="button" class="a-btn a-btn--ghost a-btn--sm a-hookrow__remove"
                                data-testid="hook-remove"
                                aria-label="@($"Remove hook {at + 1} of {name}")"
                                @onclick="() => OnRemove.InvokeAsync(new HookAt(name, at))">Remove</button>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </ListRow>
    }
}

@if (Editable)
{
    <div class="a-stack a-stack--inset a-stack--gap-4">
        <SectionHead Title="Add a hook">
            <Subtitle>
                The point decides what the action may be. A before-hook runs in the transaction and may
                only refuse or patch the row; a <code class="a-mono">beforeDelete</code> may only refuse,
                because the row is going and a patch against it would be discarded.
            </Subtitle>
        </SectionHead>

        @if (_refusal is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <Refusal data-testid="hook-refusal">@_refusal</Refusal>
        }

        <Field Label="When" LabelId="hook-points-label">
            <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="HookBuilder.Points" Selected="[_hook.Point]" aria-labelledby="hook-points-label"
                       data-testid="hook-points" SelectedChanged="points => Choose(points[0])" />
        </Field>

        <Field Label="Only when (optional)" For="hook-condition">
            <ChildContent>
                <input class="a-input a-mono" id="hook-condition" value="@_hook.Condition" autocomplete="off"
                       placeholder="@_hook.Example"
                       @oninput="args => _hook.Condition = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
            </ChildContent>
            <Hint>
                CEL over @((MarkupString)_hook.Images) and <code class="a-mono">@@user</code>. Left empty, the hook
                runs on every write.
            </Hint>
        </Field>

        <Field Label="Then" LabelId="hook-actions-label">
            <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="_hook.Kinds" Selected="[_hook.Kind]" aria-labelledby="hook-actions-label"
                       data-testid="hook-actions" SelectedChanged="kinds => _hook.Kind = kinds[0]" />
        </Field>

        @switch (_hook.Kind)
        {
            case HookBuilder.Reject:
                <Field Label="Refuse with" For="hook-reject">
                    <ChildContent>
                        <input class="a-input" id="hook-reject" value="@_hook.RejectMessage" autocomplete="off" maxlength="500"
                               placeholder="A completed work order cannot be reopened."
                               @oninput="args => _hook.RejectMessage = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
                    </ChildContent>
                    <Hint>
                        Becomes the <code class="a-mono">detail</code> of the RFC 7807 problem the caller
                        reads, so write it for them rather than for the log.
                    </Hint>
                </Field>
                break;
            case HookBuilder.Mutate:
                <div class="a-field-row">
                    <Field Label="Field" For="hook-mutate-field">
                        <input class="a-input a-mono" id="hook-mutate-field" value="@_hook.MutateField" autocomplete="off"
                               placeholder="completed_on"
                               @oninput="args => _hook.MutateField = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
                    </Field>
                    <Field Label="Set to" For="hook-mutate-value">
                        <ChildContent>
                            <input class="a-input a-mono" id="hook-mutate-value" value="@_hook.MutateValue" autocomplete="off"
                                   placeholder="now()"
                                   @oninput="args => _hook.MutateValue = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
                        </ChildContent>
                        <Hint>
                            A CEL expression, stored as <code class="a-mono">{"$cel": "…"}</code>. Wrap it in
                            quotes for a literal string.
                        </Hint>
                    </Field>
                </div>
                break;
            case HookBuilder.Webhook:
                <Field Label="Endpoint" For="hook-endpoint">
                    <ChildContent>
                        <input class="a-input a-mono" id="hook-endpoint" value="@_hook.Endpoint" autocomplete="off"
                               placeholder="dispatch"
                               @oninput="args => _hook.Endpoint = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
                    </ChildContent>
                    <Hint>
                        A name declared under <code class="a-mono">webhooks.endpoints</code>, not a URL — the
                        managed path is the one this build delivers on.
                    </Hint>
                </Field>
                break;
            case HookBuilder.Email:
                <div class="a-field-row">
                    <Field Label="Template" For="hook-template">
                        <input class="a-input a-mono" id="hook-template" value="@_hook.Template" autocomplete="off"
                               placeholder="work_order_completed"
                               @oninput="args => _hook.Template = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
                    </Field>
                    <Field Label="To" For="hook-to">
                        <input class="a-input a-mono" id="hook-to" value="@_hook.To" autocomplete="off"
                               placeholder="{{new.contact_email}}"
                               @oninput="args => _hook.To = args.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" />
                    </Field>
                </div>
                break;
        }

        <div class="a-row">
            <button type="button" class="a-btn a-btn--primary a-btn--sm" data-testid="hook-add"
                    @onclick="AddAsync">Add the hook</button>
        </div>
    </div>
}
